issues.problematic_code_snippet:
if (ret == AOF_OPEN_ERR || ret == AOF_FAILED || ret == AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED) {
    goto cleanup;
}

findings.body:
`valid_up_to` starts at 0 (src/aof.c:1488). It is updated only after an AOF command is replayed (line 1661). When the base file starts with an RDB preamble, the preamble is loaded at lines 1539-1553, and `valid_up_to` is not set there. If the first AOF command after the preamble is malformed, `fmterr` computes `sb.st_size - 0`. For a small file under `aof-load-broken-max-size`, `truncate(aof_filepath, 0)` (line 1728) empties the file, deleting the RDB preamble.

::callout{type="warning"}
With the default `aof-use-rdb-preamble yes`, a dataset whose preamble is under 4096 bytes, plus a garbage line after it (for example, a partially written tail after power loss), will start with the keys in memory and an empty AOF on disk. The next restart loads nothing, and the log says "AOF loaded anyway".

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1725-1728"}

The same missing-offset bug already exists in the `uxeof` path under `aof-load-truncated` (line 1694). This PR extends the exposure to the format-error path. No test covers a preamble base, so the bug goes unnoticed.

Fix: after the preamble loads, set `valid_up_to` to the current offset, as in the suggestion below.

::suggestion{file="src/aof.c" lines="1550-1551"}
            loadingAbsProgress(ftello(fp));
            last_progress_report_size = ftello(fp);
            valid_up_to = ftello(fp); /* The RDB preamble is a well-formed prefix. */

The test at tests/integration/aof.tcl lines 715-719 gives `wait_for_log_messages` two patterns, "AOF loaded anyway" and "Fatal error: the truncated file is not the last file". `wait_for_log_messages` in tests/support/util.tcl returns as soon as any one pattern matches, so the first line alone satisfies the test. The first pattern is printed for the base file before the non-last check runs, so the test does not verify the fatal error at all, and it would pass even if the fatal check were removed.

::code{file="tests/integration/aof.tcl" lines="715-719"}

The assertion needs to check that both lines appear, or that the server exits, rather than matching on any one of them.

The new comment at redis.conf lines 1532-1533 says the option applies "when the AOF file is corrupted in the middle". The code only ever truncates from the first malformed command to EOF, so the threshold is measured from that point. Any valid commands after the bad one are discarded, even if they are intact and the total is under the limit. The comment should say this plainly, since it is the main operational risk of enabling the option.

::diff{file="redis.conf" lines="1532-1543"}

The text also says the option handles truncation "more aggressively than aof-load-truncated". That is accurate, but it should state that the byte limit counts everything after the first bad command.
